nswer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{8}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-323879/field-5" :answer-field/id #uuid "83470186-c747-5e68-b3c0-c91b4b3315c9" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-323879/field-5/d427b944e26709d5f365fc6b5711be004a0a3509f614cbfd4943dc70d3f6d84e" :answer-field/choices ["q-323879/field-5/d427b944e26709d5f365fc6b5711be004a0a3509f614cbfd4943dc70d3f6d84e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-323879" :question/id #uuid "3c43cef2-6108-5c40-8950-aec33f6df6b0" :question/math-academy-id "q-323879" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=3x-4y,\\,y(1)=2\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the modified Euler method. Complete the first row of the table below, given that the step size is $Δx=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} | {{field-5}} |" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,{y}_{0}=y({x}_{0}),\n$$\n\nthe modified Euler method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Predictor} & : & Δ{y}_{p} & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx, \\\\ \\text{Corrector} & : & Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{p}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${y}_{p}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})$ is the initially predicted slope at the predicted new point $({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})=(x+Δx,y+Δ{y}_{p}).$\n\nFirst, we compute the value of ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =3x-4y \\\\  & =3\\cdot 1-4\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-5$ |  |  |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δ{y}_{p}$ using the predictor equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{y}_{p} & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(-5)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this value to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-5$ | $-\\frac{5}{2}$ |  |  |  |\n\nThe coordinates of the initially predicted new point given by the predictor equation are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{3}{2}, \\\\ {y}_{p} & =y+Δ{y}_{p}=2+(-\\frac{5}{2})=-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add the second value to the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-5$ | $-\\frac{5}{2}$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ |  |  |\n\nSo, the predicted slope at this point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p}) \\\\  & =3\\cdot {x}_{\\text{new}}-4\\cdot {y}_{p} \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}-4\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-5$ | $-\\frac{5}{2}$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{13}{2}$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{p}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(-5+\\frac{13}{2})\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed first row is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-5$ | $-\\frac{5}{2}$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{13}{2}$ | $\\frac{3}{8}$ |" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-323879/field-1" "q-323879/field-2" "q-323879/field-3" "q-323879/field-4" "q-323879/field-5"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6defc05f-0323-58b2-906c-c926cb3a64a5"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-323879"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-324062"] :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2xy,\\,y(1)=1\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the modified Euler method. What entry should be placed in the cell indicated in the table below, if the step size is $Δx=1?$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n| $2$ | $\\,?\\,$ |  |  |  |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,{y}_{0}=y({x}_{0}),\n$$\n\nthe Modified Euler Method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Predictor} & : & Δ{y}_{p} & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx, \\\\ \\text{Corrector} & : & Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{p}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${y}_{p}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})$ is the initially predicted slope at the predicted new point $({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})=(x+Δx,y+Δ{y}_{p}).$\n\nFirst, we compute the value of ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2xy \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $2$ |  |  |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δ{y}_{p}$ using the predictor equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{y}_{p} & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $2$ |  |  |  |\n\nThe coordinates of the initially predicted new point given by the predictor equation are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2, \\\\ {y}_{\\text{pred}} & =y+Δ{y}_{\\text{pred}}=1+2=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add the second value to the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $2$ | $3$ |  |  |\n\nSo, the predicted slope at this point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =2\\cdot {x}_{\\text{new}}\\cdot {y}_{p} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $2$ | $3$ | $12$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{p}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2+12)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 14 \\\\  & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $2$ | $3$ | $12$ | $7$ |\n\nThe value of $x$ in the next row is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=2.$ To get the value of $y$ in the next row, we add $Δy$ to $y:$\n\n$$\n{y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy=1+7=8.\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $2$ | $3$ | $12$ | $7$ |\n| $2$ | $8$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(2)≈8.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-330987/field-1/b13ac151352392d38026eed875b911708c50c896d6c09463410ac041030163ff" :answer/id #uuid "f348df4b-b642-5336-9788-95d3d36fce0d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-330987/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "3486faf1-9a11-5e64-bb84-0d1189508771" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-330987/field-1/b13ac151352392d38026eed875b911708c50c896d6c09463410ac041030163ff" :answer-field/choices ["q-330987/field-1/b13ac151352392d38026eed875b911708c50c896d6c09463410ac041030163ff"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-330987/field-2/8e918182341451051ce2dca07cfe2b17fa3abe4a925a8df02308f239a4dd798f" :answer/id #uuid "c256ea43-163b-5c63-9521-5000f4c4178c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-330987/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "ab04664e-30aa-5139-b096-6343762d260c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-330987/field-2/8e918182341451051ce2dca07cfe2b17fa3abe4a925a8df02308f239a4dd798f" :answer-field/choices ["q-330987/field-2/8e918182341451051ce2dca07cfe2b17fa3abe4a925a8df02308f239a4dd798f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-330987/field-3/4e2cb386499e1926f074a7bcd422a63175a237204a3823f0fa6c86b56d301873" :answer/id #uuid "695dbc1e-1c97-59cb-8e87-79827989c218" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-330987/field-3" :answer-field/id #uuid "a068734a-c0a0-5568-b5f5-8f28c8332142" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-330987/field-3/4e2cb386499e1926f074a7bcd422a63175a237204a3823f0fa6c86b56d301873" :answer-field/choices ["q-330987/field-3/4e2cb386499e1926f074a7bcd422a63175a237204a3823f0fa6c86b56d301873"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-330987/field-4/4280bcd0914e40dbdc039bc97ee5d5baafd2654e75e9b692316d9c28c4842856" :answer/id #uuid "d9aebbb0-a7e6-542d-b979-57fccbcf8928" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-330987/field-4" :answer-field/id #uuid "e0051e39-4786-5537-93c5-55e34070068a" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-330987/field-4/4280bcd0914e40dbdc039bc97ee5d5baafd2654e75e9b692316d9c28c4842856" :answer-field/choices ["q-330987/field-4/4280bcd0914e40dbdc039bc97ee5d5baafd2654e75e9b692316d9c28c4842856"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-330987/field-5/aa640bdf1bd5b2b15c3dbfe770f68b41d304d610e4d1af786c1f1372fe3dc48f" :answer/id #uuid "549694e0-be9f-576e-9dd2-f50fe44f9243" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{8}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-330987/field-5" :answer-field/id #uuid "a795641a-f644-5f18-9245-a1029ac42d5c" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-330987/field-5/aa640bdf1bd5b2b15c3dbfe770f68b41d304d610e4d1af786c1f1372fe3dc48f" :answer-field/choices ["q-330987/field-5/aa640bdf1bd5b2b15c3dbfe770f68b41d304d610e4d1af786c1f1372fe3dc48f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-330987" :question/id #uuid "58bf3c3e-efec-5f9d-bc33-1a1f8e84f343" :question/math-academy-id "q-330987" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2x-3y,\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the modified Euler method. Complete the first row of the table below, given that the step size is $Δx=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} | {{field-5}} |" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,{y}_{0}=y({x}_{0}),\n$$\n\nthe modified Euler method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Predictor} & : & Δ{y}_{p} & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx, \\\\ \\text{Corrector} & : & Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{p}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${y}_{p}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})$ is the initially predicted slope at the predicted new point $({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})=(x+Δx,y+Δ{y}_{p}).$\n\nFirst, we compute the value of ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2x-3y \\\\  & =2\\cdot 0-3\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-3$ |  |  |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δ{y}_{p}$ using the predictor equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{y}_{p} & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(-3)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this value to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-3$ | $-\\frac{3}{2}$ |  |  |  |\n\nThe coordinates of the initially predicted new point given by the predictor equation are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}, \\\\ {y}_{p} & =y+Δ{y}_{p}=1+(-\\frac{3}{2})=-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add the second value to the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-3$ | $-\\frac{3}{2}$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ |  |  |\n\nSo, the predicted slope at this point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot {x}_{\\text{new}}-3\\cdot {y}_{p} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}-3\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-3$ | $-\\frac{3}{2}$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{5}{2}$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{p}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(-3+\\frac{5}{2})\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed first row is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-3$ | $-\\frac{3}{2}$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{5}{2}$ | $-\\frac{1}{8}$ |" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-330987/field-1" "q-330987/field-2" "q-330987/field-3" "q-330987/field-4" "q-330987/field-5"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6defc05f-0323-58b2-906c-c926cb3a64a5"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-330987"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-331699/field-1/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "689b5db9-e5dd-5d19-88c5-cd9b36058629" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-331699/field-1/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "81cb5e46-f18b-5e39-81b8-adbb07a01366" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-331699/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "ef539a41-9510-501d-8fe8-a1ae063ed23e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-331699/field-1/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer-field/choices ["q-331699/field-1/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" "q-331699/field-1/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-331699" :question/id #uuid "dec7196b-fc58-5c64-8213-434829791945" :question/math-academy-id "q-331699" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=4x-y,\\,y(0)=2\n$$\n\nUse the modified Euler method with one step to approximate $y(1).$\n\n$y(1)≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,{y}_{0}=y({x}_{0}),\n$$\n\nthe modified Euler method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Predictor} & : & Δ{y}_{p} & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx, \\\\ \\text{Corrector} & : & Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{p}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${y}_{p}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})$ is the initially predicted slope at the predicted new point $({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})=(x+Δx,y+Δ{y}_{p}).$\n\nSince we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=1,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=1-0=1.\n$$\n\nFirst, we add the initial condition data to a table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $2$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nNow, we compute the value of ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =4x-y \\\\  & =4\\cdot 0-2 \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $2$ | $-2$ |  |  |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δ{y}_{p}$ using the predictor equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{y}_{p} & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $2$ | $-2$ | $-2$ |  |  |  |\n\nThe coordinates of the initially predicted new point given by the predictor equation are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+1=1, \\\\ {y}_{p} & =y+Δ{y}_{p}=2+(-2)=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add the second value to the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $2$ | $-2$ | $-2$ | $0$ |  |  |\n\nSo, the predicted slope at this point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =4{x}_{\\text{new}}-{y}_{p} \\\\  & =4\\cdot 1-0 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $2$ | $-2$ | $-2$ | $0$ | $4$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{p}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}((-2)+4)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $2$ | $-2$ | $-2$ | $0$ | $4$ | $1$ |\n\nThe value of $x$ in the next row is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=1.$ To get the value of $y$ in the next row, we add $Δy$ to $y:$\n\n$$\n{y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy=2+1=3\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $2$ | $-2$ | $-2$ | $0$ | $4$ | $1$ |\n| $1$ | $3$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(1)≈3.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-331699/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c8c377bf-8ccb-58c6-aea5-0a67b8cfac9d"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-331699"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-331662/field-1/7f2aea1619f9c4cbe5384e0826442d82ebdb1ac614452244ced54b63c0c77efe" :answer/id #uuid "a042c5f9-c138-5d82-be5a-b42d3348a95a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-331662/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "e2070238-28cb-58af-bc48-9392f4a1f115" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-331662/field-1/7f2aea1619f9c4cbe5384e0826442d82ebdb1ac614452244ced54b63c0c77efe" :answer-field/choices ["q-331662/field-1/7f2aea1619f9c4cbe5384e0826442d82ebdb1ac614452244ced54b63c0c77efe"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-331662" :question/id #uuid "66bf2a44-e110-5c20-9371-e474e9510692" :question/math-academy-id "q-331662" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{2}y+y,\\,y(-1)=2\n$$\n\nUse the modified Euler method with one step to approximate $y(0).$\n\n$y(0)≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,{y}_{0}=y({x}_{0}),\n$$\n\nthe modified Euler method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Predictor} & : & Δ{y}_{p} & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx, \\\\ \\text{Corrector} & : & Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{p}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${y}_{p}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})$ is the initially predicted slope at the predicted new point $({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})=(x+Δx,y+Δ{y}_{p}).$\n\nSince we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=0,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=0-(-1)=1.\n$$\n\nFirst, we add the initial condition data to a table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $2$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nNow, we compute the value of ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={x}^{2}y+y \\\\  & =(-1)^{2}\\cdot 2+2 \\\\  & =2+2 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $2$ | $4$ |  |  |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δ{y}_{p}$ using the predictor equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{y}_{p} & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =4\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $2$ | $4$ | $4$ |  |  |  |\n\nThe coordinates of the initially predicted new point given by the predictor equation are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=-1+1=0, \\\\ {y}_{p} & =y+Δ{y}_{p}=2+4=6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add the second value to the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $2$ | $4$ | $4$ | $6$ |  |  |\n\nSo, the predicted slope at this point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ={x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}{y}_{p}+{y}_{p} \\\\  & ={0}^{2}\\cdot 6+6 \\\\  & =0+6 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $2$ | $4$ | $4$ | $6$ | $6$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{p}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(4+6)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 10 \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $2$ | $4$ | $4$ | $6$ | $6$ | $5$ |\n\nThe value of $x$ in the next row is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=0.$ To get the value of $y$ in the next row, we add $Δy$ to $y:$\n\n$$\n{y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy=2+5=7.\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $2$ | $4$ | $4$ | $6$ | $6$ | $5$ |\n| $0$ | $7$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(0)≈7.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-331662/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c8c377bf-8ccb-58c6-aea5-0a67b8cfac9d"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-331662"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-331079/field-1/7f2aea1619f9c4cbe5384e0826442d82ebdb1ac614452244ced54b63c0c77efe" :answer/id #uuid "12180122-4271-5f57-a34c-98dd70a134df" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-331079/field-1/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer/id #uuid "ec8428a2-b099-51bb-9af0-daf8d7c602d6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-331079/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "c528ff74-ea7b-50b9-8fb7-38055797d04e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-331079/field-1/7f2aea1619f9c4cbe5384e0826442d82ebdb1ac614452244ced54b63c0c77efe" :answer-field/choices ["q-331079/field-1/7f2aea1619f9c4cbe5384e0826442d82ebdb1ac614452244ced54b63c0c77efe" "q-331079/field-1/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-331079" :question/id #uuid "7f84d9ed-03cc-55a2-b31f-f11a50483aa2" :question/math-academy-id "q-331079" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=y(x+2),\\,y(-1)=2\n$$\n\nUse the modified Euler method with one step to approximate $y(0).$\n\n$y(0)≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,{y}_{0}=y({x}_{0}),\n$$\n\nthe modified Euler method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Predictor} & : & Δ{y}_{p} & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx, \\\\ \\text{Corrector} & : & Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{p}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${y}_{p}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})$ is the initially predicted slope at the predicted new point $({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})=(x+Δx,y+Δ{y}_{p}).$\n\nSince we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=0,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=0-(-1)=1.\n$$\n\nFirst, we add the initial condition data to a table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $2$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nNow, we compute the value of ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(x+2) \\\\  & =2(-1+2) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $2$ | $2$ |  |  |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δ{y}_{p}$ using the predictor equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{y}_{p} & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $2$ | $2$ | $2$ |  |  |  |\n\nThe coordinates of the initially predicted new point given by the predictor equation are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=-1+1=0, \\\\ {y}_{p} & =y+Δ{y}_{p}=2+2=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add the second value to the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $2$ | $2$ | $2$ | $4$ |  |  |\n\nSo, the predicted slope at this point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ={y}_{p}({x}_{\\text{new}}+2) \\\\  & =4(0+2) \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $2$ | $2$ | $2$ | $4$ | $8$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{p}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2+8)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 10 \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $2$ | $2$ | $2$ | $4$ | $8$ | $5$ |\n\nThe value of $x$ in the next row is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=0.$ To get the value of $y$ in the next row, we add $Δy$ to $y:$\n\n$$\n{y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy=2+5=7\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $2$ | $2$ | $2$ | $4$ | $8$ | $5$ |\n| $0$ | $7$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(0)≈7.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-331079/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c8c377bf-8ccb-58c6-aea5-0a67b8cfac9d"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-331079"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-331656/field-1/e16546b53c6e5f397b7358c73b72022cb3114afdeb5e1548b7449beaacf2844f" :answer/id #uuid "e7592047-8751-50c7-8ad3-9589a94bed9b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{91}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-331656/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "2ad1c778-919a-56df-a240-3ce6f39100bd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-331656/field-1/e16546b53c6e5f397b7358c73b72022cb3114afdeb5e1548b7449beaacf2844f" :answer-field/choices ["q-331656/field-1/e16546b53c6e5f397b7358c73b72022cb3114afdeb5e1548b7449beaacf2844f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-331656" :question/id #uuid "145d5fb0-b1f7-5f8e-bf97-5746b1a521bb" :question/math-academy-id "q-331656" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x(2xy+3y),\\,y(1)=1\n$$\n\nUse the modified Euler method with one step to approximate $y(2).$\n\n$y(2)≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,{y}_{0}=y({x}_{0}),\n$$\n\nthe modified Euler method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Predictor} & : & Δ{y}_{p} & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx, \\\\ \\text{Corrector} & : & Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{p}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${y}_{p}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})$ is the initially predicted slope at the predicted new point $({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})=(x+Δx,y+Δ{y}_{p}).$\n\nSince we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=2,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=2-1=1.\n$$\n\nFirst, we add the initial condition data to a table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nNow, we compute the value of ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x(2xy+3y) \\\\  & =1(2\\cdot 1\\cdot 1+3\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $5$ |  |  |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δ{y}_{p}$ using the predictor equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{y}_{p} & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =5\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $5$ | $5$ |  |  |  |\n\nThe coordinates of the initially predicted new point given by the predictor equation are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2, \\\\ {y}_{p} & =y+Δ{y}_{p}=1+5=6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add the second value to the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $5$ | $5$ | $6$ |  |  |\n\nSo, the predicted slope at this point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ={x}_{\\text{new}}(2{x}_{\\text{new}}{y}_{p}+3{y}_{p}) \\\\  & =2(2\\cdot 2\\cdot 6+3\\cdot 6) \\\\  & =84.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $5$ | $5$ | $6$ | $84$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{p}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(5+84)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 89 \\\\  & =\\frac{89}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $5$ | $5$ | $6$ | $84$ | $\\frac{89}{2}$ |\n\nThe value of $x$ in the next row is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=2.$ To get the value of $y$ in the next row, we add $Δy$ to $y:$\n\n$$\n{y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy=1+\\frac{89}{2}=\\frac{2}{2}+\\frac{89}{2}=\\frac{91}{2}.\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $5$ | $5$ | $6$ | $84$ | $\\frac{89}{2}$ |\n| $2$ | $\\frac{91}{2}$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(2)≈\\frac{91}{2}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-331656/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c8c377bf-8ccb-58c6-aea5-0a67b8cfac9d"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-331656"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-331650/field-1/478cbf1ea235fbfb5b60a0837fffa0a678f83a3bcb8f958a024b0760ea2175ce" :answer/id #uuid "61a38e71-ca67-55ff-87fa-cf09fe5d2c40" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "11" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-331650/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "54cf41a5-4013-5cfc-86ca-bc84e8066e81" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-331650/field-1/478cbf1ea235fbfb5b60a0837fffa0a678f83a3bcb8f958a024b0760ea2175ce" :answer-field/choices ["q-331650/field-1/478cbf1ea235fbfb5b60a0837fffa0a678f83a3bcb8f958a024b0760ea2175ce"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-331650" :question/id #uuid "e0118072-75d8-5a5d-afa4-96523d4ce5d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-331650" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x(xy+y),\\,y(1)=1\n$$\n\nUse the modified Euler method with one step to approximate $y(2).$\n\n$y(2)≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,{y}_{0}=y({x}_{0}),\n$$\n\nthe modified Euler method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Predictor} & : & Δ{y}_{p} & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx, \\\\ \\text{Corrector} & : & Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{p}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${y}_{p}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})$ is the initially predicted slope at the predicted new point $({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})=(x+Δx,y+Δ{y}_{p}).$\n\nSince we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=2,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=2-1=1.\n$$\n\nFirst, we add the initial condition data to a table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nNow, we compute the value of ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x(xy+y) \\\\  & =1(1\\cdot 1+1) \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $2$ |  |  |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δ{y}_{p}$ using the predictor equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{y}_{p} & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $2$ |  |  |  |\n\nThe coordinates of the initially predicted new point given by the predictor equation are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2, \\\\ {y}_{p} & =y+Δ{y}_{p}=1+2=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add the second value to the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $2$ | $3$ |  |  |\n\nSo, the predicted slope at this point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ={x}_{\\text{new}}({x}_{\\text{new}}{y}_{p}+{y}_{p}) \\\\  & =2(2\\cdot 3+3) \\\\  & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $2$ | $3$ | $18$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{p}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2+18)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 20 \\\\  & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $2$ | $3$ | $18$ | $10$ |\n\nThe value of $x$ in the next row is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=2.$ To get the value of $y$ in the next row, we add $Δy$ to $y:$\n\n$$\n{y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy=1+10=11.\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $2$ | $3$ | $18$ | $10$ |\n| $2$ | $11$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(2)≈11.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-331650/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c8c377bf-8ccb-58c6-aea5-0a67b8cfac9d"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-331650"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-21277"] :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' = 2xy + y^2, \\qquad y(0)=1\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the modified Euler method. Given that the step size is $\\Delta x = 1,$ fill the missing entries in the table below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $y'$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ |  |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y_0 = y(x_0),\n$$\n\nthe predictor equation of the modified Euler method with step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{y}_{p}={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe initially predicted new $y$-value is then given by $y_p = y + \\Delta y_p.$\n\nFirst, we compute the value of $y'$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2xy+{y}^{2} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 0\\cdot 1+{1}^{2} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute $\\Delta y_p$ using the predictor equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{y}_{p} & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this value to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $1$ |  |\n\nHence, according to the predictor equation, the initially predicted new $y$-value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =y+Δ{y}_{p} \\\\  & =1+1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed table is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $2$ |" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-21560"] :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' = xy^2 - x^2, \\qquad y(0)=2\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the modified Euler method. Complete the first row of the table below, given that the step size is $\\Delta x = \\dfrac12.$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $2$ | $0$ | $0$ | $2$ | $\\dfrac{7}{4}$ |  |" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y_0 = y(x_0),\n$$\n\nthe modified Euler method with step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Predictor} & : & Δ{y}_{p} & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx, \\\\ \\text{Corrector} & : & Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{p}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $y'_p = f(x_\\text{new},y_p)$ is the initially predicted slope at the predicted new point $(x_\\text{new}, y_p) = (x + \\Delta x, y + \\Delta y_p).$\n\nWe compute $\\Delta y$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{p}^{′})Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(0+\\frac{7}{4})\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(\\frac{7}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed first row is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $2$ | $0$ | $0$ | $2$ | $\\dfrac{7}{4}$ | $\\dfrac{7}{16}$ |" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-21274"] :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' = xy - y^2 + 2, \\qquad y(0)=1\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the modified Euler method. Complete the first row of the table below, given that the step size is $\\Delta x = 1.$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ |  |  |  |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y_0 = y(x_0),\n$$\n\nthe modified Euler method with step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Predictor} & : & Δ{y}_{p} & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx, \\\\ \\text{Corrector} & : & Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{p}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $y'_p = f(x_\\text{new},y_p)$ is the initially predicted slope at the predicted new point $(x_\\text{new}, y_p) = (x + \\Delta x, y + \\Delta y_p).$\n\nFirst, we compute the value of $y'$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =xy-{y}^{2}+2 \\\\  & =0\\cdot 1-{1}^{2}+2 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ |  |  |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute $\\Delta y_p$ using the predictor equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{y}_{p} & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this value to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $1$ |  |  |  |\n\nThe coordinates of the initially predicted new point given by the predictor equation are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+1=1, \\\\ {y}_{p} & =y+Δ{y}_{p}=1+1=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add the second value to the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $2$ |  |  |\n\nSo, the predicted slope at this point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p}) \\\\  & ={x}_{\\text{new}}{y}_{p}-{y}_{p}^{2}+2 \\\\  & =(1)(2)-(2)^{2}+2 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $0$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $\\Delta y$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{p}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1+0)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed first row is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $0$ | $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ |" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-21275"] :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' = x^2y^2+xy^2, \\qquad y(1)=1\n$$\n\nUse the modified Euler method with one step to approximate $y(2).$" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y_0 = y(x_0),\n$$\n\nthe modified Euler method with step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Predictor} & : & Δ{y}_{p} & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx, \\\\ \\text{Corrector} & : & Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{p}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $y'_p = f(x_\\text{new},y_p)$ is the initially predicted slope at the predicted new point $(x_\\text{new}, y_p) = (x + \\Delta x, y + \\Delta y_p).$\n\nSince we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=2,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = 2 - 1 = 1.\n$$\n\nFirst, we add the initial condition data to a table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nNow, we compute the value of $y'$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={x}^{2}{y}^{2}+x{y}^{2} \\\\  & ={1}^{2}\\cdot {1}^{2}+1\\cdot {1}^{2} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $2$ |  |  |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute $\\Delta y_p$ using the predictor equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{y}_{p} & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $2$ |  |  |  |\n\nThe coordinates of the initially predicted new point given by the predictor equation are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2, \\\\ {y}_{p} & =y+Δ{y}_{p}=1+2=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add the second value to the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $2$ | $3$ |  |  |\n\nSo, the predicted slope at this point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ={x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}{y}_{p}^{2}+{x}_{\\text{new}}{y}_{p}^{2} \\\\  & ={2}^{2}\\cdot {3}^{2}+2\\cdot {3}^{2} \\\\  & =54.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $2$ | $3$ | $54$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $\\Delta y$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{p}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2+54)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 56 \\\\  & =28\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $2$ | $3$ | $54$ | $28$ |\n\nThe value of $x$ in the next row is $x_\\text{new} = 2.$ To get the value of $y$ in the next row, we add $\\Delta y$ to $y{:}$\n\n$$\ny_\\text{new} = y + \\Delta y = 1 + 28 = 29\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ | $y'_p$ | $\\Delta y$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $2$ | $3$ | $54$ | $28$ |\n| $2$ | $29$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(2) \\approx \\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{29}.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
